WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:02.050 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  police and emergency workers cordoned  off an area in central Paris after a  gunman went on a deadly shooting rampage  firing at people in a hair salon Kurdish  restaurant and Kurdish Community Center  eyewitnesses said people ran in panic  we were walking down the street we heard  shots we turned around people were  running left and right after five or six  minutes we went into the hairdressers  and we saw people had stopped a man he  was quite old three people were injured  in the salon  foreign  police arrested a 69-year-old Frenchman  he was known to authorities and had been  previously charged with racist violence  against migrants living in tents in the  city  the individual may have attacked these  tents an investigation started at the  end of 2021 and the individual has  recently been released as news spread of  the shooting people from the city's  Kurdish Community gathered to protest  is not random the killer did it to scare  the Kurdish Community  we don't know how we will go on North  Kurdistan is dangerous but we live in  France in the capital and now it's  dangerous here France's prison condemned  what he called in an odious attack  against Kurds in France earlier the  interior Minister had said the shooter's  motivation was unclear  he was clearly targeting foreigners we  need to know from the judicial inquiry  presided over by the public prosecutor  whether it was specifically for the  Kurds but for the moment there is no  evidence to show that  there was no doubt though in the minds  of demonstrators angry and upset they'd  burned flags and threw rocks  the police have moved in now to try and  clear the protesters because things were  becoming quite violent  the protests subsided but police  remained on standby and investigation's  been opened into the attack and the  interior Ministry says Security will be  increased at Kurdish sites in the  country for some they say it's a welcome  measure but one that comes too late  Natasha Butler Al Jazeera Paris

